# Written for the VS Code REST Client extension (humao.rest-client).
#
# Tokens are pulled straight out of the login responses with
# {{requestName.response.body.$.token}} -- nothing to copy or paste. Run request 2
# and 3 once, then everything below resolves.
#
# NOTE: in IntelliJ's HTTP Client this syntax does not work. There you would capture
# the token in a `> {% client.global.set(...) %}` response handler instead.

@host = http://localhost:8080/api

### 1. Register. Always creates a CLIENT -- no field here could ask for HOST.
POST {{host}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "newgolfer@example.com",
  "username": "newgolfer",
  "password": "1234"
}

### 2. Log in as a client. Requests below reference this response.
# @name clientLogin
POST {{host}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "zach",
  "password": "1234"
}

### 3. Log in as the host.
# @name hostLogin
POST {{host}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "tourpro",
  "password": "1234"
}

### 4. Wrong password. 401 WITH a message -- and it never says which half was wrong.
POST {{host}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "zach",
  "password": "not-it"
}

### 5. Who am I. Run request 2 first.
GET {{host}}/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{clientLogin.response.body.$.token}}

### 6. Trade a valid token for a fresh one. The app calls this on launch.
GET {{host}}/auth/refresh
Authorization: Bearer {{clientLogin.response.body.$.token}}

### 7. No token at all. 401 with an EMPTY body -- this one is supposed to fail.
GET {{host}}/auth/me

### 8. A token that is not a token. Also 401, empty, no stack trace.
GET {{host}}/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer not-a-real-token

### 9. The host's identity, confirming the role claim rides along.
GET {{host}}/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{hostLogin.response.body.$.token}}
